News for the tz database

Release 2026f - 2026-10-27 23:16:49 -0700

  Briefly:
    Cuba will not fall back 2026-11-01; stays on -04 through 2027-11-07.
    No more leap seconds, and leap second tables no longer expire.
    Improve 1965/1966 data for Brazil.

  Changes to future timestamps

    Cuba will not fall back to -05 on 2026-11-01 and instead plans to
    maintain -04 through 2027-11-07.

  Changes to past timestamps

    In 1965 Brazil ended DST on 03-31 at 24:00 not 00:00, and in 1966
    it ended DST on 03-01 at 01:00 not 00:00.  (Thanks to Daniel Dias
    Rodrigues.)

  Changes to code

    zic now ignores Expires lines, as leap second tables in TZif files
    no longer expire now that there are no more leap seconds;
    see Internet RFC 9636 §3.2.  (Thanks to Garrett Wollman for the
    heads-up re no more leap seconds.)

    localtime.c no longer supports 49 leap seconds, as 27 now suffice.
    zic now warns if you go over the new limit.

    A new compile-time flag -DMIN_TRANSITION_GAP=N specifies a minimum
    transition gap of N seconds when reading TZif files.  It currently
    defaults to 0, but can be set to 1 if TZif files generated by zic
    2021b and earlier are no longer of concern.

    With 32-bit time_t, localtime.c functions now check for transition
    ordering in TZif data as carefully as they do with 64-bit time_t.
    (Problem reported by Ana Bobillo.)

    On platforms like FreeBSD that have O_RESOLVE_BENEATH but lack
    O_REGULAR, localtime.c compiled with -DOPENAT_TZDIR now checks
    that a TZ setting with ".." components names a regular file
    before opening it in an unprivileged program, as it already did
    for other settings that might name a file outside TZDIR.
    This fixes a bug introduced in 2026c.  (Thanks to Ana Bobillo.)

  Changes to documentation and commentary

    Documentation and commentary now reflect leap seconds’ demise.

  Changes to commentary

    Discussion on timekeeping in northwestern Ontario has been updated
    to reflect that provincial regulation has been issued to allow these
    areas to stay aligned with America/Winnipeg and follow Manitoba’s
    adoption of year-round -05 after all.  (Thanks to Aoun Khan,
    Chris Walton, and Heitor David Pinto.)

    Discussion on timekeeping in Saskatchewan has been updated to
    document its new Time Act, 2026 which comes into force on
    2026-10-30.  (Thanks to Heitor David Pinto.)  No change is expected.


Release 2026e - 2026-09-29 17:14:38 -0700

  Briefly:
    Manitoba moves to permanent -05 on 2026-10-31.

  Changes to future timestamps

    Manitoba’s 2026-03-08 spring forward was its last foreseeable clock
    change, as it moved to permanent -05 thereafter.  Model this with
    its traditional abbreviation EST.  Although the change to
    permanent -05 legally takes place on 2026-10-31, temporarily model
    the change to occur on 2026-11-01 at 02:00 for the same reason as
    other recent temporary hacks.  This new temporary hack works
    around a limitation in Unicode CLDR 48.2.2 (2026-09-21)
    and ICU 78.3 (2026-03-17), and is planned to be removed after
    CLDR and ICU are fixed.  (Caution: see “NOTE FOR 2026
    TEMPORARY HACK FOR CLDR AND CANADA” below.)

      As the change affects both America/Winnipeg and its backward
      compatibility link, the obsolescent setting TZ="Canada/Central"
      will now use the abbreviation EST for affected timestamps,
      akin to TZ="Canada/Pacific" behavior introduced in 2026b.

      Another TZDB release may be needed soon if any of the portions of
      northwestern Ontario historically aligned with America/Winnipeg do
      not follow.  For now, given the short lead time with Manitoba’s
      announcement, assume that they will, to avoid creating new zones
      that may not be needed if no divergence occurs.

  Changes to past timestamps

    In 1925 Ireland fell back on 09-20 not 10-04 (thanks to Stan Ulbrych).

  Changes to commentary

    Discussion on timekeeping practices in northwestern Ontario has been
    expanded in light of Manitoba’s recent announcement.  It is not yet
    known whether or to what extent these areas may adapt their own
    timekeeping practices in response.


Release 2026d - 2026-09-11 15:21:07 -0700

  Briefly:
    Canada’s Northwest Territories moved to permanent -06 on 2026-08-21.
    Obsolescent settings like TZ="EST5EDT" now conform better to POSIX.
    Fix security, performance and porting bugs in zic and localtime.

  Changes to future timestamps

    Canada’s Northwest Territories will not fall back on 2026-11-01
    and will stay on -06 year-round, matching Alberta’s recent change.
    Model this with its traditional abbreviation CST.  Although the
    change to permanent -06 legally took place on 2026-08-21,
    temporarily model the change to occur on 2026-11-01 at 02:00
    for the same reason as other recent temporary hacks.
    This new temporary hack works around a limitation in Unicode CLDR
